  "question": {
    "text": "The relation $R=\\{(x, y): x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ and $x+y$ is even $\\}$ is:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "24K7O",
      "text": "reflexive and symmetric but not transitive",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "3GcXR",
      "text": "an equivalence relation",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "dQY0c",
      "text": "symmetric and transitive but not reflexive",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "2pKTC",
      "text": "reflexive and transitive but not symmetric",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "For reflexive $(x, x) \\in \\mathbb{R}, x \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\n$\\Rightarrow x+x=2 x \\rightarrow \\text { even }$\nFor symmetric of $(x, y) \\in \\mathbb{R}$ then $(y, x) \\in \\mathbb{R}$ when $x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\n$x+y \\rightarrow$ even\n$\\Rightarrow y+x \\rightarrow \\text { even }$\nfor transitive if $(x, y) \\in \\mathbb{R} \\Rightarrow x+y \\rightarrow$ even\n$(y, z) \\in \\mathbb{R} \\Rightarrow y+z \\rightarrow \\text { even }$\n$x+2 y+z \\rightarrow$ even\n$\\Rightarrow x+z$ is even\n$\\Rightarrow(x, z) \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is an equivalence relation.",
    "image": null
  },
